Ladybird shapes text-overflow’s three periods in whichever font of
the block has a period for them, and keeps the ellipsis character only
where the block’s first available font has it. That’s what Gecko’s
gfxFontGroup::MakeEllipsisTextRun() and Blink’s LineTruncator::
SetupEllipsis() do: Both choose U+2026 only if the first font has it,
and shape the result with font fallback. WebKit draws U+2026 from a
fallback font instead. Checked in Chrome 158.0.8127.37, Firefox 151.0,
and WebKit 26.5, with Ahem limited to the space and capital letters
by unicode-range: Chrome and Firefox draw three periods from the next
font, and WebKit an ellipsis.
